In addition to Regional Governments, prevention efforts as referred to in Article 11 paragraph (2) shall be carried out by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions; and/or
d. Non-Governmental Organizations.

Article 2
(1) The Regional Government is responsible for implementing efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence and protect victims of violence.
(2) Prevention of the occurrence of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:
a. collecting data and information about women and children as well as related laws and regulations;
b. conducting education on anti-violence values; and
c. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of prevention and protection of victims of violence.
(3) Protection of victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:
a. establishing and facilitating the operation of integrated service institutions for victims by involving community elements;
b. facilitating assistance, legal aid and legal services according to the needs of victims;
c. providing housing both safe houses and alternative housing along with handling mechanisms, services, psychosocial and spiritual;
d. conducting continuous handling up to the rehabilitation and social reintegration stage;
e. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection of victims of violence; and
f. encouraging public awareness of the importance of protection for victims of violence.
(4) In order to implement the responsibility as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall establish programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in one Regional Action Plan for the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence.
(5) The Regional Action Plan for the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (4) shall be established for a period of 3 (three) years by Decree of the Mayor.

Article 41
(1) The implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence is carried out by the Women and Children Protection Service Center as an integrated Service Center to provide protection, handling and fulfillment of the rights of victims of violence.
(2) The Service Center as referred to in paragraph (1) is an implementing unit under the coordination of the Department and constitutes an integrated technical service unit that functions as an information center for women and children, a service center for women and children victims of violence as well as an empowerment center for women and children.
(3) The implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. providing fast and free services to victims;
b. organizing complaint services, consultation, mediation, outreach, health, social rehabilitation, spiritual guidance, assistance, enforcement and legal aid, repatriation, social reintegration;
c. conducting cooperation with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants needed for victims;
d. networking with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of victims as well as conducting cooperation with witness and victim protection institutions, social protection houses or trauma centers owned by the government, community or other institutions for health recovery of victims;
e. providing convenience, comfort and safety for victims;
f. maintaining confidentiality of victims; and
g. providing fulfillment of legal aid for victims.
(4) The institutional structure of the Women and Children Protection Service Center can be upgraded to a structural institution in the form of a Technical Implementation Unit of the Department (UPTD) as long as it meets the criteria and requirements for the establishment of a Technical Implementation Unit of the Department according to the provisions of the Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs.
(5) Further provisions regarding the establishment, institutional structure, procedures and mechanisms for organizing the Women and Children Victims of Violence Protection Service Center are regulated by a Mayor Regulation.

Article 5
Regional governments are obligated to implement policies, programs and activities for the protection of women, which can be carried out through efforts to:
a. provide and facilitate protection services for women;
b. develop a data system for the protection of women;
c. implement coordination of the implementation of policies, programs and activities for the protection of women among Regional Apparatus; and
d. conduct cooperation with the business sector, non-governmental institutions, private companies, Foundations, and other domestic institutions as well as international institutions, in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 21
Regional Governments are obligated to implement prevention of violence against children which includes:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/or
f. other violence.

Article 6
(1) The provision and facilitation of protection services for women as referred to in Article 5 letter a shall be in the form of:
a. victim identification;
b. legal assistance;
c. medical rehabilitation;
d. psychosocial rehabilitation;
e. social reintegration; and
f. return assistance;
(2) The provision and facilitation of protection services for women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided to women:
a. victims of violence;
b. victims of human trafficking;
c. elderly women who are not independent;
d. women with disabilities;
e. victims of sexual exploitation;
f. heads of household;
g. in conflict-prone and disaster areas;
h. workers; and
i. other vulnerable groups.

Article 7
Every female victim of violence has the right to receive:
a. protection and assistance;
b. legal aid;
c. health services according to medical needs;
d. spiritual services;
e. psychosocial services;
f. information; and
g. integrated services.

Article 19
(1) Regional Governments are obligated to implement the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the field of social welfare, which includes:
a. children in conflict with the law;
b. child victims of violence who are exploited economically and sexually, neglected children, child victims of abduction and child victims of human trafficking;
c. child victims of narcotics abuse, alcohol, psychotropic substances and other addictive substances (NAPZA);
d. children infected with HIV/AIDS;
e. children who do not have parents and are cared for by other parties or families who are unable;
f. street children;
g. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters;
h. children with disabilities;
i. children of migrant worker families;
j. children living in or around prostitution locations; and/or
k. child victims of other maltreatment.
(2) Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of provision of services:
a. health;
b. education;
c. legal aid;
d. assistance;
e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation;
g. empowerment;
h. social assistance; and/or
i. reintegration.
